{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["And you shall keep — as I explained to you, the reason for the matter." "This day, to do them — in the aggadah of Yelamdenu on the passage כִּי מִי גוֹי גָּדוֹל: Rabbi Oshaia said: who is like this nation, that knows the temperament (אופי) of its Elohim — they know what He asks of them and they do it; they know how He cherishes them, etc. The Arukh explained: אופי — the nature of His mind. Addendum in Shokher Tov, psalm למנצח בנגינות: Rabbi Ze'iri said: a man who has a household servant — the first time he enters, he seats him on the bed; the second, on the chair; the third, on the bench; the fourth, he says: how this so-and-so presses upon me and burdens me! But the Holy One, blessed be He — all Israel press Him with prayer, and He listens and rejoices; therefore it says: מי כַּיהוָה אֱלֹהֵינוּ בְּכָל קָרְאֵנוּ אֵלָיו. Rabbi Yitzchak said in the name of Rabbi Yehudah: idol worship seems close, as it says: יִשָּׂאֵהוּ עַל כָּתֵף יִסְבְּלוּהוּ — but it is far: לְבַסּוֹף אַף יִצְעַק אֵלָיו וְלֹא יַעֲנֶה. But the Holy One, blessed be He, seems far — as you say: from the earth to the firmament is five hundred years, and likewise between each firmament and the next, etc. — yet a man enters the synagogue and whispers, and the Holy One, blessed be He, hears his prayer, as it says: בְּכָל קָרְאֵנוּ אֵלָיו. In Yelamdenu, parashat אִם שָׁמֹר תִּשְׁמְרוּן: there are 248 words in the Qeriat Shema that guard the 248 limbs. And further in Yelamdenu and in Pesikta de-Shav Or Kesev: a parable of a king who sent hisgrama to a province — they took it and stood on their feet and bared their heads and read it with awe, terror, and trembling. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: Qeriat Shema is my grama. And likewise in Pesikta de-BaChodesh HaShlishi: let not the words of Torah's aggadah be in your eyes like an old grama. The Arukh explained: grama — a letter."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {1 "פרוזדוגמא/פרוזבול transliterated here as 'grama' following the abbreviated form in the Hebrew text; the term is Greek προσγραφή (prographē), a written decree or proclamation. The citations of Shokher Tov and Pesikta abbreviate the full titles as in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:38:37.635999229Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}